{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWZmTA3fBeuqDQCUafpG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012N29mgFWhTHwbZ8EkpeyHW","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"summary":"I wrote the three missing documents: `design/implementation-design.md`, `design/test-design.md` and `test-execution-plan.json`. They were absent before, and they are the only files I changed. This is my own authored design work, not acceptance.\n\n**Implementation design** (`sha256:355e130d…e27f`)\n- Takes `specification/project-conformance.md` (`sha256:220ed4e3…9d52`) as its authority, as the original stage requires.\n- Defines the CLI in seven small decisions: one file at `generated/hello-world.mjs`, run as `node generated/hello-world.mjs` with no arguments, one `console.log(\"Hello, world!\")` call, no imports and a normal exit.\n- Pins the output: `Hello, world!` plus one newline, 14 bytes, with hex and digest given, and exit status 0.\n- Records the fix history: the original CLI (`647697a5…`) printed the greeting without the comma, and both tests failed. The fault was one missing comma, so a one-line edit to the CLI was enough. The corrected CLI (`fe8f4080…`) passed both tests. The old failure stays attached to the old file.\n- Names both supplied test files, with digests, as the verification surfaces, and maps each requirement R-1 to R-15 to the design and its check. The current CLI's bytes match the designed one-line program exactly.\n\n**Test design** (`sha256:84594a0b…93f6`)\n- Specifies two checks: the component test (`CLI process satisfies its output contract`) and the user acceptance test (`a user receives the exact promised greeting`). Each runs the real CLI and asserts exit status 0 and exact stdout.\n- Adds a direct run of the CLI as the check behind the plan's expected return value.\n- Reviews both supplied test files line by line against the original instructions. Both meet them.\n- Shows the tests are not vacuous: between the recorded failing and passing runs, only the CLI's digest changed, and the result flipped from 0 passed / 2 failed to 2 passed / 0 failed.\n- Includes a table of 14 kinds of faulty CLI output and which checks would catch each. I confirmed the output and exit-status cases in memory with the same strict assertions, without touching any file.\n- Sets the rule that both named tests must pass in the same run, plus the acceptance rule, which evidence counts, known limitations and what invalidates the design.\n\n**Execution plan** (`sha256:320a5511…cd8c`)\n- Top-level fields as the original requires: `command` `\"node\"`, the exact test arguments, `expectedTestPassCount` 2, `expectedStdoutMatch` `\"fail 0\"` and `assertedReturnValue` `\"Hello, world!\"`.\n- Declares both fixed commands with their expected results, and names both required tests with their file digests.\n- Defines `observedTestPassCount` and `planSatisfied`, and the rule: accept only if the test command exits 0, at least 2 tests pass, and the plan is satisfied. Results come from the recorded command runs, not from a written `test-execution-result.json`.\n- Pins the digests of every source and document it depends on. It states that the plan runs nothing and is not where the commands come from.\n\n**My checks** (not recorded evidence)\n- The plan parses, meets the conformance criteria for the plan (AC-7), and agrees with both fixed commands.\n- Every digest it pins matches the current files.\n- I applied the acceptance rule to the recorded output of both earlier test runs: the original CLI's run is rejected (0 passed, 2 failed) and the corrected CLI's run is accepted (2 passed, 0 failed, both named tests passed).\n- The original task, oracle, witness contract, both test files, the CLI and the conformance document all still match their recorded digests.","gaps":["No independent assessment has reviewed the three new documents or the plan's match to the sources (AC-5, AC-6, AC-7, AC-9, AC-12). How well they fit and whether they are complete is my own judgment only.","My checks — the plan's criteria, digest matching, the acceptance rule applied to recorded output, and the in-memory faulty-output checks — are not recorded evidence. When I applied the acceptance rule to the original CLI's failing test run, I used a shortened copy of its output with the stack traces removed; parsing does not depend on them.","I did not run either command. The latest recorded runs (`46f72954…` and `69ff716a…`) are on the current CLI digest `fe8f4080…`. Whether they are fresh enough for the final assessment, and whether a fresh installed Public run and replay are needed, is not my decision.","The plan pins the digests of the conformance document and both design files. Revising any of them makes the plan stale for its basis, and it would need updating. The test design likewise pins the implementation design's digest.","Some limitations remain open: the recorded runs don't capture the Node version or which `node` binary ran (U-3), the tests depend on running from the project root (U-4), neither test sets its own timeout, and stderr and argument handling are unconstrained (U-1, U-2). The original CLI's bytes are known only from its digest, its recorded output and the author's report (U-7).","The mutation checks were done in memory, not by altering the CLI, because that is outside my write scope. Only the missing-comma case is backed by recorded evidence.","The four conflicts between the original task and the witness contract (C-1 to C-4) are applied as the case authority resolves them. I don't own those decisions.","All seven parent obligations remain open. This work contributes evidence toward the implementation-design, test-design and execution-plan obligations; it does not close them."]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2913,"cache_read_input_tokens":95731,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2913},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b03af36-2775-4615-a95c-3d13324ea1b2","uuid":"0e9a730e-70d0-48b6-a265-16bc3d462b35","timestamp":"2026-09-28T21:42:40.629Z","request_id":"req_011CfWZmSLgLgAz2MFQQF6ri","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012N29mgFWhTHwbZ8EkpeyHW":{"summary":"I wrote the three missing documents: `design/implementation-design.md`, `design/test-design.md` and `test-execution-plan.json`. They were absent before, and they are the only files I changed. This is my own authored design work, not acceptance.\n\n**Implementation design** (`sha256:355e130d…e27f`)\n- Takes `specification/project-conformance.md` (`sha256:220ed4e3…9d52`) as its authority, as the original stage requires.\n- Defines the CLI in seven small decisions: one file at `generated/hello-world.mjs`, run as `node generated/hello-world.mjs` with no arguments, one `console.log(\"Hello, world!\")` call, no imports and a normal exit.\n- Pins the output: `Hello, world!` plus one newline, 14 bytes, with hex and digest given, and exit status 0.\n- Records the fix history: the original CLI (`647697a5…`) printed the greeting without the comma, and both tests failed. The fault was one missing comma, so a one-line edit to the CLI was enough. The corrected CLI (`fe8f4080…`) passed both tests. The old failure stays attached to the old file.\n- Names both supplied test files, with digests, as the verification surfaces, and maps each requirement R-1 to R-15 to the design and its check. The current CLI's bytes match the designed one-line program exactly.\n\n**Test design** (`sha256:84594a0b…93f6`)\n- Specifies two checks: the component test (`CLI process satisfies its output contract`) and the user acceptance test (`a user receives the exact promised greeting`). Each runs the real C